  "image_name": "21ad534944bea6b6c365563f7d00dbe3.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts healthy soil with degraded soil beneath crop plants, showing how soil condition affects plant growth and belowground function. In the healthy soil, the structure is shown as well organized, with macroaggregates, microaggregates, macropores, and strong pore connectivity. This supports a larger, more vigorous plant. In degraded soil, particles appear more dispersed, pores are disconnected, and the plant is visibly smaller.\n\nThe middle portion emphasizes connectivity. Healthy soil allows deeper root penetration, a more extensive mycorrhizal network, better water infiltration and distribution, and greater oxygen movement through the soil. In degraded soil, roots are shallow and sparse, the fungal network is underdeveloped, water seems to run off rather than infiltrate, and oxygen penetration is reduced.\n\nThe bottom portion highlights nutrient and gas cycling. Healthy soil is associated with richer biodiversity, active metabolism, efficient nutrient turnover and availability, methane oxidation, nitrification, and lower nutrient leaching. Degraded soil appears biologically poorer and less efficient, with reduced nutrient cycling, greater runoff and nutrient loss, and more emissions of methane and nitrous oxide. Overall, the figure shows that good soil structure underpins connected biological processes that sustain plant productivity.",
